PART 4: PAYMENT PARAMETER
11. payment_ever_done
This parameter checks whether the customer's account has a record in the payment data.
The code effectively creates:
payment_ever_done = Yes
if the account number exists in the payment table.
Otherwise:
payment_ever_done = No
Example
Account exists in payment history
Account Number: 12345
Payment History: Found
Result:
payment_ever_done = Yes
Account doesn't exist
Account Number: 67890
Payment History: Not Found
Result:
payment_ever_done = No
Where is it used?
Primarily here:
Result: Release Held Goods-fraud check payment done
Logic:
Agent did NOT decline
       +
No previous payment
       ↓
Amber Error

PART 5: eligibility
12. eligibility
This is the technical filter deciding whether the call gets sent to the AI.
It requires all of the following:
Level 2 is Store Referral - Authorised
OR Store Referral - Declined

AND

account_type = credit

AND

Queues = Voice Credit Referral
If all are true:
eligibility = 1
Otherwise:
eligibility = 0
Main usage
Eligibility = 1
      ↓
Create AI Prompt
      ↓
Send Transcript to AI
Eligibility = 0
      ↓
Do not meaningfully process through
the Release Held Goods AI flow

PART 6: AI PARAMETERS
The AI analyses the transcript and answers a series of questions.
These AI-generated parameters are extremely important because the scoring rules depend on them.

13. Is the call from a store about authorization code/store card referral?
This is effectively the conversation-level applicability check.
The AI reads the transcript and determines:
Yes
or
No
Why is it important?
Many rules begin with:
Is this a relevant store referral call?
If:
No
then many subsequent rules become:
No Error
Important distinction
This is different from:
eligibility
eligibility uses metadata.
This parameter uses the actual transcript.

PART 7: DPA PARAMETERS
14. Full name/customer number confirmation
The AI checks whether the agent obtained or confirmed the required customer identification information.
This contributes to:
Result: Release Held Goods-DPA Check

15. Value of sale
The AI checks:
Did the agent ask for the value of the sale?
This is also used in the DPA-related logic.

16. Store number/ID
The AI checks whether the agent obtained the relevant store identification.
Again, this contributes to:
DPA Check

PART 8: PRODUCT AND ORDER PARAMETERS
17. Gift Card
AI parameter:
Did the agent ask if the order contains a Gift Card?
Possible answers:
Yes
No
Result impact
Relevant Call = Yes
+
Gift Card Question = No
↓
Amber Error

18. Store Card
AI parameter:
Did the agent ask if customer is using store card for retail order?
This determines whether the agent established the payment method.
Error
Relevant Call = Yes
+
Store Card Question = No
↓
Amber Error

19. Electrical / Branded Goods
The AI checks whether the order involves:
Electrical goods
OR
Relevant branded goods
This information drives the electrical goods-related checks.

20. Multiple Electrical Goods
The AI determines whether the agent established if there were:
Multiple electrical goods
This check is more conditional than the basic electrical goods check.
It can depend on:
Relevant call
+
Store card usage
+
Electrical/branded goods
+
Payment history

21. Large Furniture
The AI checks whether the order is for:
Large furniture items only
Why?
Because large furniture can follow a different decision path from other goods.

22. Order Value Exceeds £500
The AI checks whether the relevant order exceeds:
£500
This is an important branching parameter.
Conceptually:
Order
  ↓
Is it large furniture?
  ↓
If not, does value exceed £500?
The £500 threshold then affects subsequent checks.

PART 9: CUSTOMER VERIFICATION PARAMETERS
23. Accessible Mobile
The AI checks whether the agent established that the customer has access to their registered mobile number.
This becomes relevant in the applicable high-value order scenario.
Result
If required but not checked:
Amber Error

24. PIN Verification
The AI checks whether the agent requested or completed PIN verification.
Again, this becomes applicable under the relevant conditions.
If required but not performed:
Amber Error

PART 10: TRANSACTION DECISION PARAMETERS
25. Did the agent decline the transaction?
This is one of the most important AI parameters.
Possible values:
Yes
No
It influences multiple parts of the final logic.

Why is transaction decline important?
A declined transaction can change the expected Release Held Goods journey.
For example:
Agent declined transaction
may prevent certain checks from being considered an error.
It is also used in the payment fraud rule:
No previous payment
+
Agent did not decline
↓
Amber Error
But:
No previous payment
+
Agent declined
↓
No Error

PART 11: SIGNATURE PARAMETER
26. Signature at Till Receipt
The AI checks:
Did the agent remind the store consultant to get the customer to sign the till receipt?
When applicable
Generally when the transaction was not declined.
Error classification
Instead of Amber or Red, the code uses:
Compliant with development
This is effectively a lower-priority development/compliance issue.

