PART 16: N.A. CONDITIONS
The code can convert the overall result into:
N.A.
under certain conditions.
Examples include when:
1. Incorrect Level 2
If the call isn't:
Store Referral - Authorised
or
Store Referral - Declined
the final result becomes:
N.A.

2. Not the final agent
If:
agent_order != agent_order_max
then:
iAudit Result = N.A.

3. AI analysis is unavailable or invalid
The downstream logic can also prevent an incomplete AI analysis from producing a normal audit result.

4. Conversation quality / completeness issues
The code also considers parameters relating to whether:
The transcript is partial
The conversation ended abruptly
The conversation is incomplete
These can affect whether the call should receive a normal audit result.

PART 17: SPECIAL FINAL OVERRIDE — DECLINED TRANSACTION
There is an important override:
If the agent declined the transaction
the final outcome may be changed to:
Good Customer Outcome
provided the conditions in the code for this override are satisfied.
This is important because it can override earlier non-critical findings.
So the final result flow is not simply:
Individual Errors → Highest Priority
There are additional business-rule overrides afterwards.

PART 18: Remarks
The code generates remarks from the individual result columns.
For every result containing an error, it creates a readable description.
Conceptually:
Amber Error: Gift card,
Amber Error: Electric Good
These are joined together.
Therefore, the final remarks explain:
Which Release Held Goods checks caused the audit outcome.

